WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.794
Topological sorting orders the vertices of a directed acyclic graph so every directed edge points forward in the sequence.

00:00:08.794 --> 00:00:16.290
Prerequisite tasks, course schedules, and dynamic programming transitions all rely on topological order.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:21.480
If the graph contains a directed cycle, no valid linear ordering exists.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:28.333
Kahn's algorithm tracks the indegree of every vertex and pushes all zero-indegree starters into a queue.

00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:36.816
Pop a vertex, append it to the ordering, and decrement the indegrees of its outgoing neighbors, enqueuing any neighbor that hits zero.

00:00:36.816 --> 00:00:41.310
If fewer than V nodes are emitted, a cycle blocked the remaining nodes.

00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:51.183
When a problem asks for the lexicographically smallest valid topological order, replace Kahn's FIFO deque with a min-heap.

00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:56.376
Always pop the smallest available zero-indegree vertex label next.

00:00:56.376 --> 00:01:03.143
Heap operations add only a logarithmic factor while enforcing the exact priority rule.

00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:10.236
Once you have the topological array, any path-counting or longest-path query becomes a single forward loop.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:16.228
Iterate vertices in topological order and push each node's DP value across its outgoing edges.

00:01:16.228 --> 00:01:22.028
Acyclicity guarantees every incoming transition is finalized before a vertex pushes onward.
